# Go language rules

Installed by P/D-1.

Framework version: 0.1.7

Cite `go/C-n`; apply framework authority and review gates.

## Common rules

- go/C-1 (must) — Rule: Keep source and standard-library usage compatible with
  the module's declared Go version and supported build configurations.
  Exception: Newer-version files may use appropriate version build constraints
  when older supported builds retain a working implementation.
- go/C-4 (must) — Rule: Handle or return errors that can affect the operation's
  result; justify intentional discards when the reason is not evident or documented.
- go/C-5 (must) — Rule: Use `errors.Is` or `errors.As` for cause checks that must
  survive wrapping. Exception: Match error text when that text is the contract.
- go/C-6 (should) — Rule: Add error context only when it identifies useful
  operation or input information absent from the cause.
- go/C-7 (must) — Rule: Return errors for expected operational failures.
  Exception: Documented `Must` APIs may panic on invalid programmer-supplied input.
- go/C-21 (must) — Rule: Preserve cause inspection required by the API; use `%w`
  when adding text. Do not expose a cause that the API contract hides.

## Load by impact

Evaluate every trigger against changed code and affected callers/contracts.
Load matching files before writing or reviewing; expand as impact emerges.
Paths are relative to this file. Do not load research during ordinary tasks.

| Impact | Load |
| --- | --- |
| API/type contracts, package structure, exported names, dependency selection, or optimization | `rules/go/design.md` |
| Contexts, blocking operations, resources, networking, or transactions | `rules/go/io.md` |
| Goroutines, channels, shared mutable state, or synchronization | `rules/go/concurrency.md` |
| Choosing Go test coverage or writing/reviewing affected Go tests | `rules/go/testing.md` |

Spec reviewers account for loaded `must` rules and give one non-applicability
reason per unloaded file. A missing required file is missing input.

## Checks

Use project commands without duplicating covered checks. Fallback: build affected
packages, `gofmt -l` changed files, `go vet`, and `go test -race`; include affected
consumers. Unsupported `-race`: run ordinary tests and report the verification gap.
Module changes require module-wide build/test and `govulncheck ./...`.

Run configured linters only. Bound targeted fuzz/benchmark runs.
-- repo/docs/solomonar/skills/go/rules/go/concurrency.md --
# Go concurrency

- go/C-9 (must) — Rule: Give started goroutines an explicit owner and completion
  or shutdown path; observe completion before releasing resources they use.
  Exception: Process-lifetime work may end with its owning process.
- go/C-10 (must) — Rule: Before closing a channel, establish that no sender can
  send again and no other closer can close it. Channels need not be closed.
- go/C-11 (should) — Rule: Keep critical sections limited to the protected
  invariant; move I/O and callbacks outside locks when that invariant permits.
- go/C-35 (must) — Rule: Synchronize shared state when concurrent accesses include a write.
- go/C-36 (should) — Rule: Bound input-driven concurrency and queued work according
  to the operation's resource budget.
- go/C-37 (must) — Rule: Do not copy used locks, wait groups, or other synchronization
  structs that prohibit copying, including through enclosing structs.
-- repo/docs/solomonar/skills/go/rules/go/design.md --
# Go design

- go/C-17 (should) — Rule: Choose names that explain their purpose at the call
  site, accounting for package and receiver context.
- go/C-22 (should) — Rule: Introduce interfaces, generic APIs, or helper layers
  only for a current caller, test boundary, or concrete simplification; retain
  small duplication when extraction obscures independent behavior.
- go/C-23 (should) — Rule: Define needed interfaces around the consumer's used
  methods. Exception: Shared protocols may own interfaces; returning an interface
  is appropriate when it is the intended contract.
- go/C-24 (should) — Rule: Export only the API consumers need; keep state private
  when direct mutation would bypass its invariants.
- go/C-25 (must) — Rule: Preserve consumed API signatures and observable contracts,
  including errors and serialized values, unless the task requires their change.
- go/C-26 (must) — Rule: Use pointer receivers when field assignments must update
  the original instance or the receiver must not be copied.
- go/C-27 (should) — Rule: Document new or changed exported APIs and packages,
  including non-obvious ownership, cancellation, and error contracts.
- go/C-28 (should) — Rule: Justify performance-specific complexity with a relevant
  measurement and compare the change against its baseline.
- go/C-29 (should) — Rule: Prefer the standard library or an established project
  dependency when it meets the requirements without substantial custom machinery.
- go/C-30 (should) — Rule: Place behavior in a cohesive package with a meaningful
  API; introduce a package split only for a concrete responsibility or dependency boundary.
-- repo/docs/solomonar/skills/go/rules/go/io.md --
# Go contexts and I/O

- go/C-8 (must) — Rule: Accept `context.Context` first in project-controlled
  cancellable APIs and propagate it to blocking work.
  Exception: Preserve imposed signatures; obtain context where their contract
  permits and document cancellation limits.
- go/C-12 (must) — Rule: Arrange release of owned resources and derived-context
  cancel functions on every exit path; make ownership transfers explicit.
- go/C-13 (must) — Rule: Ensure owned transactions reach commit or rollback;
  report success only after `Commit` succeeds. Deferred rollback is valid cleanup.
- go/C-14 (should) — Rule: Release per-iteration resources within the iteration
  when retaining them until function return would accumulate live resources.
- go/C-15 (must) — Rule: Bound finite network operations with effective deadlines
  or timeouts, including caller-provided bounds.
  Exception: Long-lived streams require lifecycle cancellation and appropriate
  phase or idle limits instead of a finite total duration.
- go/C-31 (must) — Rule: Pass context per operation rather than storing it in
  structs. Exception: An imposed interface may require storing it.
- go/C-32 (must) — Rule: Use context values for request-scoped metadata, not
  dependencies or optional function parameters.
- go/C-33 (must) — Rule: Check `sql.Rows.Err()` after iteration before reporting success.
- go/C-34 (should) — Rule: Choose server phase and idle timeouts for the protocol
  and deployment, accounting for limits already enforced by infrastructure.
-- repo/docs/solomonar/skills/go/rules/go/testing.md --
# Go testing

Framework procedures own acceptance coverage, test-first workflow, and verification gates.

- go/C-18 (must) — Rule: Use controlled inputs and event synchronization so
  results do not depend on wall-clock delays, random fixtures, or scheduling.
- go/C-19 (should) — Rule: Mark test helpers with `t.Helper()` when failure
  locations should point to their callers.
- go/C-38 (should) — Rule: Make failures identify the scenario, actual result,
  and expected result without requiring a second run.
- go/C-39 (should) — Rule: Use tables for cases sharing test logic; use subtests
  when independent names, lifecycle, or filtering help. Separate cases whose
  setup or assertions would require branch-heavy table machinery.
- go/C-40 (must) — Rule: Isolate mutable state before parallelizing tests;
  do not call `t.Setenv` or `t.Chdir` under parallel ancestry.
- go/C-41 (should) — Rule: Tie fixtures to test lifetime with `t.TempDir()` and
  `t.Cleanup()`; use `t.Context()` for test-lifetime cancellation on Go 1.24+.
  Exception: Use a separately scoped context when exercising cancellation or
  when cleanup must continue after the test context is canceled.
- go/C-42 (should) — Rule: Consider fuzzing input-heavy code with a meaningful
  property; keep targets deterministic, isolated, and inexpensive.
